+   restarts is the difference between a diagnostic and a foundation.
+2. **Base-frame pacing.** The limiter frame generation will need is useful on its
+   own and verifiable now. Sleep to within a millisecond, spin the rest; a long
+   stall does not produce a burst of catch-up frames.
+3. **Honest reporting.** It publishes exactly which inputs it has proven and
+   explicitly masks off the ready bits it has not earned. `PublishStatus()`
+   clears those bits every frame, by construction. The interface cannot unlock
+   2x/3x on the strength of a wish.
+
+It also fixes a leak v2 had: `g_depth_state` was keyed by command list and never
+erased, so a long session with many deferred contexts grew it without bound and
+kept dangling keys. v3 handles `destroy_command_list`.
+
+---
+
+## Installation
+
+One entry point. Five phases, and the first mutation is in phase four:
+
+```
+DISCOVER   find the game, the GPU, what is already installed
+PREFLIGHT  check every precondition, print a verdict
+PLAN       decide the exact operations, print them
+APPLY      execute, journalling each one before it happens
+VERIFY     re-check from disk, independently of the plan
+```
+
+The user is not asked to choose between "RP Visual" and `Full Neural`. The
+installer works out what the machine can run, says what it chose and why, and
+`-Mode` remains for anyone who wants to override it.
+
+**Every operation is journalled before it happens**, with the SHA-256 of what it
+replaced and where that was backed up. A crash between two operations leaves a
+journal that still describes exactly what had already happened. Uninstall replays
+it backwards — an exact inverse rather than "move the folder back and hope".
+
+`-WhatIf` stops after PLAN. VERIFY deliberately re-reads from disk instead of
+consulting the plan it just ran, because a check that shares a data structure
+with the installer only proves the installer is self-consistent.
+
+---
+
+## What is verified, and what is not
+
+**Verified here.** `core/` and `app/` build clean under
+`-Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -Wshadow -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion`, and 57
+assertions pass — the evidence ladder, decay, dependency clamping, `AnyOf`
+groups, blocker propagation, the full arbitration sweep, the look curves, base-FPS
+arithmetic, planner purity, malformed-input handling, badge truthfulness and the
+kernel loop driven by fake probes and effectors.
+
+**Type-checked only.** `platform/`, `probe/`, `effect/`, `ui/`, `addon_main.cpp`
+and the input provider compile clean with `-Wall -Wextra` against stub headers in
+`tests/stubs/`, which catch syntax and type errors but are not the real Windows
+SDK, ReShade add-on API or ImGui. Real signatures must be confirmed by the MSVC
+build inside the RenoDX tree.
+
+**Not verified at all.** The PowerShell has not been executed — no PowerShell
+host was available where it was written. It needs a run against a real FiveM
+installation before release.
+
+**Not implemented.** Geometry motion injection, HUD-less capture, UI separation,
+frame constants and generated-frame delivery. Frame generation stays locked, and
+the code is structured so that it cannot be unlocked by accident.
+
+---
+
+## Attribution
+
+This control layer is SECRET EMKO's. Nothing under it is.
+
+- ReShade add-on API — Patrick Mours / crosire, BSD-3-Clause
+- RenoDX — Carlos Lopez Jr. and contributors, MIT
+- DLSS 5 Bridge — NIGos and contributors, MIT
+- The live-control adapter derives from the MIT DLSS5-Swapper UI bridge
+- NVIDIA DLSS and Streamline — NVIDIA, under NVIDIA's own terms
+
+The adapter remains fail-closed against one exact pinned build: size, SHA-256,
+code fingerprints and ImGui dispatch identity must all match. A different build
+falls back to restart-based configuration rather than guessing at offsets. That
+was the right call in v2 and it is unchanged.
+
+No PureDark code, binaries, authentication or paid assets are used. Public
+statements about GTA V's lack of engine motion vectors are used as architectural
+reference only.
diff --git a/v3/docs/BUDGET.md b/v3/docs/BUDGET.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..6a8b588
--- /dev/null
+++ b/v3/docs/BUDGET.md
@@ -0,0 +1,448 @@
+# Performance, latency, stability, quality
+
+Four things get traded against each other, and improving one routinely damages
+another. This document states which is which, what each currently costs, and
+what the code does about it.
+
+The target is not "faster than the alternative". It is: **on every axis, not
+worse — and where a cost is inherent, say the number instead of hiding it.**
+
+## The order
+
+```
+1. Stability      never negotiable
+2. Quality        the picture is the product
+3. Performance    frames, within the budget quality leaves
+4. Latency        paid last, and stated openly
+```
+
+Stability is first in every profile: the alternative is a crash or a confidently
+wrong picture, and nobody would accept that trade knowingly. The three below it
+follow the priority the player picks — `Quality`, `Balanced` or `Performance` —
+and every derived number comes from that one choice rather than from a dozen
+sliders.
+
+Latency is last **by design, not by neglect**. The cost is real, it is computed,
+and it is printed next to the control that incurs it. Accepting it knowingly is
+a legitimate choice; discovering it afterwards is not.
+
+## Native resolution is the cheap win
+
+At render scale 1.0 the neural pass is not reconstructing anything — it is
+antialiasing. That is worth separating out because it changes what the work is:
+
+- it is the **best-looking** mode, since no pixels are being invented;
+- and it needs **none of the render-target resizing** reconstruction does.
+
+Everything that makes upscaling invasive — intercepting resource creation,
+rescaling scene targets, fixing post-process chains that assume fixed sizes — is
+absent at 1.0. What remains is jitter injection and switching the game's own
+temporal AA off. That is a small fraction of the work for most of the quality.
+
+So the `Quality` profile is both the best result and by far the least risky
+thing to build. Reconstruction at 0.667 and 0.5 remains available for players
+who need the headroom, and is the harder engineering.
+
+## Pacing
+
+A base rate that does not divide into the display rate produces judder that no
+frame counter can show. 70 fps doubled onto a 120 Hz panel repeats a frame
+roughly every seventh — the classic "high frame rate that still feels wrong".
+
+`EvaluatePacing()` names it and `PacingAdvice()` says which base rate would fix
+it. The reference configuration — 60 capped, doubled, onto 120 Hz — lands
+exactly, and the interface says so.
+
+```
+tests/test_budget.cpp
+  ok   sixty_doubled_onto_a_hundred_and_twenty_hertz_paces_exactly
+  ok   a_base_rate_that_does_not_divide_the_display_is_called_out
+  ok   producing_more_frames_than_the_display_can_show_is_called_out
+tests/test_planner.cpp
+  ok   a_hundred_and_twenty_hertz_at_native_with_a_sixty_cap_comes_out_right
+```
+
+---
+
+## 1. Latency
+
+This is the axis most likely to be quietly worse, so it comes first.
+
+### The inherent cost
+
+Frame generation makes input latency worse. Not through poor implementation —
+by construction.
+
+To display an interpolated frame between rendered frames N and N+1, N+1 must
+already exist. So the entire output sequence is delayed by one **rendered**
+frame:
+
+| Display | Multiplier | Base rate | Added lag |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 144 Hz | 2x | 72 | **+13.9 ms** |
+| 144 Hz | 3x | 48 | **+20.8 ms** |
+| 120 Hz | 2x | 60 | +16.7 ms |
+| 60 Hz | 2x | 30 | +33.3 ms |
+
+Note the second row. **3x is worse for latency than 2x**, because it renders at
+a lower base rate. That is the opposite of what the numbers on the buttons
+suggest, and it is exactly the kind of thing a player should be told rather than
+left to feel.
+
+`EstimateLatency()` computes this, tests pin the arithmetic, and the Frames page
+prints it next to the 2x/3x buttons:
+
+```
++14 ms input lag
+A rendered frame has to be held back before the extra ones can be shown.
+3x holds it longer than 2x does.
+```
+
+```
+tests/test_budget.cpp
+  ok   generated_frames_cost_one_rendered_frame_of_input_lag
+  ok   three_times_is_worse_for_latency_than_two_times
+  ok   the_latency_summary_says_a_number_rather_than_a_feeling
+```
+
+### What reduces it
+
+**Reflex is not optional.** NVIDIA pairs DLSS-G with Reflex precisely because
+the held frame has to be paid for somewhere, and Reflex pays for it by keeping
+the render queue short and starting each frame as late as it safely can. Frame
+generation without it is a straightforwardly worse experience. This is a Stage 4
+requirement, listed alongside the interop layer rather than after it.
+
+**Where the limiter waits matters.** A frame-rate cap that sleeps *before*
+present adds latency; one that sleeps *after* present, as ours does, delays the
+start of the next frame instead and is roughly neutral. The genuinely best
+position is before the game samples input, which a ReShade add-on cannot reach —
+so the limiter is as good as it can be from here, and not better. Said plainly
+because the difference is real.
+
+### The honest comparison
+
+On the current path — image pass only, no generation — **added latency is
+zero**, and the base-rate limiter can slightly reduce it by keeping the present
+queue from growing. There is no latency regression to explain today. The table
+above is what switching generation on will cost when Stage 4 lands.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Performance
+
+### What the pipeline costs per frame
+
+| Pass | Work | Estimated at 1440p |
+|---|---|---|
+| Camera detection, locked | one 16-float read | negligible |
+| Camera detection, searching | see below | throttled |
+| Reprojection | one unproject + one project per pixel | ~0.1–0.2 ms |
+| Validation | 18 texture samples per pixel at full rate | **~1–2 ms** |
+| Image pass | the provider's own cost | unchanged from v2 |
+
+The validation pass is the expensive one, and it is the first thing given up.
+
+### Camera detection
+
+The naive version evaluates every 16-byte offset of every mapped buffer, twice,
+each costing a matrix inverse, a frustum extraction, an apex solve, a field-of-
+view derivation and a depth-convention probe. At a few hundred buffer maps a
+frame that is not viable.
+
+Three things fix it.
+
+**A structural gate.** For a row-vector view-projection built as `view * proj`,
+the last column is the view matrix's third column — the camera's forward axis,
+and therefore a unit vector. Three multiplies and a compare reject almost
+everything before any of the expensive work happens.
+
+Measured, on the same synthetic drive:
+
+| Buffer | Offsets examined | Full evaluations | Share |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 96 floats, a sixth matrices | 1,680 | 132 | 7.9% |
+| 1,024 floats, realistic | 20,240 | 92 | 0.45% |
+| 4,096 floats, realistic | 81,680 | 92 | **0.11%** |
+
+The camera is still found in all three.
+
+**Throttling.** While searching, the scan runs on one frame in four. The camera
+does not move far in 60 ms and the lock threshold counts scanned frames.
+
+**It stops.** Once locked, one offset in one buffer is read per frame and the
+broad scan is off entirely. Detection is a startup cost, not a running one.
+
+**And the gate can switch itself off.** An optimisation that silently rejects
+the thing being looked for is not an optimisation. A game that stores its matrix
+scaled differently fails the unit-length test — so if nothing has passed the
+gate in two seconds, the gate is assumed wrong, disabled, and the full scan
+runs. Tested both ways: the gate rejects a uniformly scaled matrix, gives up on
+itself, and then finds it.
+
+```
+tests/test_viewproj_detector.cpp
+  ok   on_a_realistic_buffer_the_gate_removes_over_ninety_nine_percent
+  ok   a_prefilter_that_would_hide_the_camera_switches_itself_off
+```
+
+Slot lookup also moved from a linear scan over up to 512 candidates to a hash
+map — that scan ran once per surviving candidate, per buffer, per frame.
+
+### The budget governor
+
+If our passes do not fit, quality is reduced in a defined order rather than
+frames being missed. A dropped frame is far more visible than a coarser
+confidence mask.
+
+```
+motion detail Full -> Half -> Quarter      (cheapest quality loss, spent first)
+3x -> 2x                                    (costs frames)
+2x -> off                                   (last resort)
+```
+
+The budget itself follows the chosen priority, because how much the pipeline may
+spend is exactly the question the priority answers:
+
+| Profile | Overhead allowed | Patience before degrading | Before recovering |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Quality | 25% or 6 ms | 40 frames | 90 frames |
+| Balanced | 15% or 4 ms | 20 frames | 120 frames |
+| Performance | 10% or 2.5 ms | 10 frames | 180 frames |
+
+Whichever is smaller, so a player already at 20 fps is not charged a percentage
+of a long frame. The *order* of what gets given up does not change between
+profiles — mask detail before frames is the least-damage order regardless — only
+how much room there is and how quickly the governor acts.
+
+Asymmetric on purpose: a tier that changes several times a second looks worse
+than either tier.
+
+```
+tests/test_budget.cpp
+  ok   mask_detail_is_the_first_thing_given_up
+  ok   the_multiplier_is_only_dropped_once_detail_is_exhausted
+  ok   a_single_expensive_frame_changes_nothing
+  ok   recovery_is_slower_than_degradation
+  ok   a_low_frame_rate_uses_the_absolute_ceiling_not_a_percentage
+```
+
+---
+
+## 3. Stability
+
+### The crash that was in the code
+
+The detector read constant buffer contents at unmap, straight from the pointer
+the map call returned. If the notification arrives after the runtime has already
+released the mapping, that is an access violation — a crash in someone else's
